<div id="speakers">
<h3>Plenary lectures</h3>
<ul>
	<li style="margin-bottom:1em;"><span class="bigtitle"><a href="https://bsantoro.ccny.cuny.edu/">Bianca Santoro (CCNY/CUNY)</a></span>
	  <br><span class="bigtitle">
	      The math behind soap films</span>  <div class="abstract">
	    <b>Abstract:</b>
	    What happens when you dip a curved wire into a soap solution?
	      The resulting soap film has the least possible area among all surfaces that bound the wire. We will try to understand the math behind those soap films, and hopefully inspire you to play with soap bubbles at home as well! If you happen to have a few spare paper clips and some dish liquid, you could start your experiments near the end of the talk.
	      <p><b><a href="https://youtu.be/OZxBtSTl00Q">Lecture video</a></b></p>
	    </div>

	    
	    	<li><span class="bigtitle"><a href="http://www-personal.umich.edu/~itobasco/">Ian Tobasco (UIC)</a></span>
      	  <br><span class="bigtitle">
	     Wrinkles and Crumples and Origami, oh my!</span>  <div class="abstract">
	  <b>Abstract:</b>
	  Crumple a sheet of paper and a seemingly unpredictable network of creases and folds appears. This talk will discuss the mathematical analysis of such crumpling patterns, their more orderly cousin origami, as well as other diffuse versions of crumpling known as "wrinkling patterns". Along the way, we will learn the basics of elasticity theory, and see how tools from geometry, analysis, and mechanics can be used to predict and categorize the zoo of patterns exhibited by thin elastic sheets.
  </div>

	    
    

